1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
PEIRA: Learning Predictive Encoders through Inter-View Regressor Alignment

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,200
Self-distillation methods such as BYOL, SimSiam and DINO train a student to predict a teacher.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,200
They work well in practice.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,200
But they do not minimize a well-defined objective, so what they learn is poorly understood.

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:19,700
And representations can collapse onto a single point.

6
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:22,900
Two views x and y of one image go through encoders U and V, which map them to k‑dimensional features.

7
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:26,900
Two covariance statistics of the features: Σ measures the signal shared across views, and N the variability within each view.

8
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:30,900
For fixed encoders, the ridge regressor that predicts each encoded view from the other has a closed form, a matrix-valued signal-to-noise ratio.

9
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:35,400
PEIRA maximizes the trace of P, with a quadratic penalty that controls feature scale.

10
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,100
Toy experiment of the paper: both methods start from a tiny initialization (ρ₀ = 10⁻⁶), so every direction starts near 0.

11
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:42,100
In the paper's toy experiment, self-distillation from a tiny initialization stays collapsed: none of the four target directions grows.

12
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:47,100
For PEIRA, collapse is unstable. Training escapes it and recovers the target directions one after another, while the non-target directions stay at 0.

13
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,100
Across initialization scales, PEIRA recovers all four target directions every time.

14
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:53,800
Each bar is √cᵢ, the square root of the i-th canonical correlation between the two views. This spectrum is illustrative.

15
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:57,800
Raising λ moves the threshold up. A direction is kept only if √cᵢ > λ; directions below the line are dropped.

16
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:01,800
Each kept direction is learned with amplitude g(cᵢ, λ) = max(√cᵢ − λ, 0)¹ᐟ², a soft threshold: amplitudes shrink as λ grows and reach zero at √cᵢ = λ.

17
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:05,800
Larger λ keeps fewer directions, so λ sets the effective dimension. λ < √c₁ is needed; otherwise every direction is filtered out.

18
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:10,500
Alignment αᵢ ∈ [0, 1] of the top eigenvectors eᵢ of the noise N_(U,V) with respect to the signal Σ_(U,V), on a single training run.

19
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:15,500
The spectrum of N_(U,V) develops additional significant modes during training, consistent with the population analysis.

20
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,500
Online linear-probe top-1 and the negative PEIRA objective −ℰ_(PEIRA) are strongly correlated during training.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:28,900
Linear-probe accuracy (Table 1): best on ImageNet-1K; on CIFAR-10, SIGReg leads and PEIRA beats VICReg.

22
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:34,900
